  "packageSize": 436443,
  "published": "2026-11-22T09:51:50.447Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Spans from NAM leases are now bound to the lease. Pooled<T>.AsSpan, PreparedPooled<T>.AsSpan, Local<T>.AsSpan and ArenaLease<T>.AsSpan return a span the compiler ties to the variable that holds the lease, so returning it, storing it in a ref struct passed by ref, assigning it to an out parameter or keeping it past the lease's block is a compile error (CS8168, CS8156 or CS8352). These escapes used to compile and could read memory after it was returned. NativeRegion.Lease and LeaseUninitialized are bound the same way, so a Local<T> cannot leave the method that owns its region.\n\nTo upgrade: declare a span local scoped when it starts as default and is assigned a lease span later (scoped Span<int> span = default;). A helper that needs a lease's elements should take the span, not the lease.\n\nAnalyzer: NAM1001, NAM1012, NAM1013, NAM1040 and NAM1050 messages name the value and the destination instead of placeholders. NAM1012 no longer reports a discarded region lease. NAM1003 no longer reports a lease that a helper takes as an in, ref or ref readonly parameter. NAM1045 and NAM1046 no longer report a Write callback that reads a primitive or enum state. NAM1027, NAM1033 and NAM1040 now also check local functions and top-level helpers.",
